# The ignore file for `Containerfile.freebsd.mcp`, and it is the strongest
# statement this repository makes about what the mcp component is.
#
# buildah has no equivalent of BuildKit's `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` lookup,
# so this one is not found by being beside the Containerfile: it is passed
# explicitly, and the build command in the Containerfile's header does so.
#
#   buildah bud --ignorefile server/oci/Containerfile.freebsd.mcp.containerignore ...
#
# It could have gone at the context root under the name `.containerignore`,
# which is what buildah looks for there.  It does not, for the reason
# `Containerfile.linux.dockerignore` gives: a test in `usage/tests/test_all.py`
# walks the repository root for container files by NAME and refuses them, on
# the grounds that a root-level container file "reads as the way to run the
# project".  `.containerignore` is not on that test's list today -- which makes
# putting one there a way of satisfying the letter of a guard while defeating
# it, and this repository has audited itself for exactly that shape more than
# once.
#
# THE BOUNDARY IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A PROMISE.
#
#   The MCP surface is an HTTP CLIENT of the read API: a reader token, no store
#   access, no account outside its scope.  Handing it the DuckDB engine or the
#   store's query layer would be a privilege escalation and would defeat the
#   split.  The Containerfile could simply not COPY them -- but then the next
#   edit could, by accident, and nothing would say so.
#
#   With this file, `server/srv/store.py`, `duck.py`, `query.py`, `api.py` and
#   `serve.py` ARE NOT IN THE BUILD CONTEXT AT ALL.  A `COPY server/srv/`
#   added here fails the build by name rather than quietly producing a fatter
#   image with a database handle in it.  That is the same reason the door's
#   read API is gated by `readers.json` rather than by being unreachable: a
#   property enforced by the shape of the thing beats one enforced by everyone
#   remembering.
#
# The context is the REPOSITORY ROOT, because the image needs `LICENCE`,
# `server/srv/mcp.py` and `server/oci/`, and no one of them is a parent of the
# others.
#
# Last match wins, so the `__pycache__` line at the bottom is what keeps a
# developer's stale byte-code out of a published image -- and it matters more
# here than it does for Linux, because this image has no RUN and therefore
# never re-compiles what it copies.
*
!/LICENCE
!/server/srv/mcp.py
!/server/oci/entrypoint-mcp.sh
!/server/oci/stage-freebsd-mcp/**
**/__pycache__
